d novice and the intuitive  and reactive expert another popular  model used by sports psychologists even  today is the process of learning motor  skills postulated by P Fitz and M Posner  in 1967 this describes three distinct  phases cognitive associative and  autonomous either way though the point  is that you must first learn the  movement then refine it until it becomes  second nature so one way we're going to  help this process along is something  called spaced learning space learning  essentially means that you're dividing  your learning into smaller blocks so  instead of sitting down to learn  something for an hour you instead break  that session into two 30-minute blocks  or four 15-minute ones usually this is  employed when learning factual  information where it's been shown  through significantly improve retention  the most popular protocol involves  repeating the same content three times  but with two ten-minute breaks in  between it's thought that this works by  allowing the memories to move from  short-term into long-term memory stores  thereby ensuring that you're actually  training your ability to recall that  information and strengthening your  access to it there's also the simple  fact that space learning provides a  break allowing you to resume your  learning feeling fresher later on as  opposed to seeing your results trail off  due to diminishing returns over time  what's really cool though is that this  approach also seems to work for motor  skill acquisition in one study  researchers attempted to train postal  workers on a new typing task they  divided participants into groups using  various different learning schedules it  was found that the spaced learning  groups learned the new tasks faster and  achieved lower error rates and this was  true even when they studied for less  total time in other words it could  theoretically also help you to improve  your punch technique or masters of a  planche more quickly if you're going to  be practicing planche progressions  part of a larger workout then it  actually seems to make sense to break up  that training into blocks but it could  just mean that you use your skill  practice at the start and at the end of  the workout then of course you'll be  more fatigued towards the end this also  makes the case for splitting training  into multiple blocks throughout the day  rather than doing all things in one go  this is something I've been  experimenting with heavily lately with  great results I'll discuss that more on  a post very soon this could even benefit  strength gains by allowing a quicker  neural adaptation of course recovery and  overtraining should be managed carefully  if practicing skills and small blocks  throughout the day sounds familiar it's  probably because you've heard of  greasing the groove greasing the groove  simply means treating strength  performance like skills you herse over  and over any opportunity without  allowing yourself to become fatigued the  classic example is that in order to  become amazing at pull-ups you should  install a pull-up bar on your door and  then do five or six every time you pass  through it the aim is not to fatigue  yourself but to gain the necessary  muscle memory every time you engage in  practice you light up those neural  networks improving your efficiency for  inter muscular coordination and muscle  fiber recruitment greasing the groove  was initially advocated by pavel chat  zulan he recommends that the volume per  session should be small and the  intensity light your practicing not  training stick between fifty to sixty  percent of your maximum effort  as a good benchmark this can be applied  to pretty much anything I've used it  with great success to improve my strict  handstand you can also use it to improve  your max jump height your punching  techniques your golf swing your mobility  or pretty much anything else if it's the  neural networks were focused on though  then perhaps you don't need the physical  movement at all simply visualizing  movement and awesome choreography can  actually trigger almost the precise same  brain activity as though you are  rehearsing it in real life you can  therefore use mental practice as a way  to further enhance greasing the groove  and spaced learning without placing more  stress on the body this makes a lot of  sense for someone like Batman who is  probably severely over trained as it is  believe it or not even watching people  perform the moves you're trying to learn  may be effective in a similar manner and  speaking of overtraining that brings us  to sleep which is an absolutely crucial  piece of the puzzle during sleep our  brain riri hearses the skills we've  practiced during the day it curls  unwanted connections and it moves data  into long-term  memory countless studies support the  notion that sleep is crucial for  procedural memory formation and  strengthening this is why more and more  athletes that focused on getting huge  amounts of sleep see the book why we  sleep by Matthew Walker of course this  is something that Batman would struggle  with due to his rather busy schedule and  I'm in a similar situation because I've  got a young daughter that makes me like  Batman right the best he could hope for  would be to maximize the quality of the  sleep he got possibly using methods to  increase sleep spindles in the necessary  brain regions the number of these high  frequency oscillations appears to  correlate with a rapid skill acquisition  and activity in the motor cortex  apparently if we create an association  between a sound and our skill practice  during the day and then play that sound  during the night we can actually  encourage more activation and spindles  in the relevant region check out the TED  talk from penny Lewis for more on this  if you're a regular viewer then you  might know that I've been experimenting  with a neuro priming device called the  halo sport - this is a transcranial  direct current stimulation 